```bash
{% build_command %}
```

This produces `target/{% rust_target %}/release/{% crate_name %}.wasm`.

Use `{% build_command %}`, not `cargo build`: the Stellar CLI selects the correct
target and attaches the contract metadata the network expects.

## Deploy

```bash
# One-time: create and fund an identity on testnet
stellar keys generate --global alice --network testnet --fund

stellar contract deploy \
  --wasm target/{% rust_target %}/release/{% crate_name %}.wasm \
  --source alice \
  --network testnet
```

The command prints a contract ID. Invoke the contract with it:

```bash
stellar contract invoke \
  --id <CONTRACT_ID> \
  --source alice \
  --network testnet \
  -- hello --to Dev
```

## Layout

```
Cargo.toml            package, contract profile settings
rust-toolchain.toml   pins the toolchain and build target
src/lib.rs            the contract
src/test.rs           tests, using the SDK test utilities
.github/workflows/    CI: fmt, clippy, test, and a real contract build
```

## Next steps

- Add functions to the `impl` block in `src/lib.rs`; the test client is
  regenerated from it automatically.
- Read up on [storage
  types](https://developers.stellar.org/docs/build/guides/storage) — this
  contract uses `instance` storage, which suits small shared state, while
  per-user data usually belongs in `persistent` storage.
